  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nTonight and Sunday:\n\nHigh pressure is moving southward into the region this \nafternoon, with light winds across northeast MN. Northerly winds\nare a bit breezier over northwest Wisconsin, where the pressure\ngradient is a bit tighter. The lake breeze along the North \nShore coupled with a weak wave aloft has allowed for a few \nshowers to develop. Although development might extend \nsouthwestward toward the head of the lake, better chances for a \nquick rain shower this afternoon are over the Arrowhead and \nnorth of Silver Bay. With loss of daytime heating, any activity \nwill quickly diminish. \n\nTonight, with continued northeast winds off the lake, there is \na 20-40% chance dense fog could spread inland, mainly along the\nSouth Shore and up the St. Louis River valley. Visibilities \ncould drop below 0.5 mile through early Sunday morning. \n\nSunday, quiet conditions expected as surface high pressure \nshifts southward over the Northland. Winds will start to turn to\nthe south but highs will remain close to seasonal averages or a\nfew degrees below. Most of the area will start out in the 50s \ntomorrow morning, with a few upper 40s over the Arrowhead. \nAfternoon temps warm into the 70s to mid 80s. Lows Sunday night \nwill reflect the warmer airmass arriving ahead of the next \nfront, with lows ranging from the lower 60s across the west to \nupper 40s over the Arrowhead. \n\n\nMonday/Tuesday:\n\nSoutherly flow increases Monday, allowing highs to climb into \nthe 80s. Dew points will also climb back into the mid and upper \n60s across out west. A shortwave, moving out of the northern\nRockies, will pivot around the base of a trough associated with\na Canadian closed low, and push a cold front into the Northland\nMonday evening. Ahead of the front, Monday afternoon, scattered\nshowers will be possible, mainly north of the Brainerd Lakes, \nas moisture and lift increases ahead of the approaching cold \nfront. Most of the area will remain dry through early evening. \n\nBetter forcing arrives with the front, and a line of strong to\nsevere storms, pushing west to east through the Northland \nMonday night into Tuesday morning. With the loss of daytime \nheating, instabilities will drop off quickly as the front \narrives. However, lapse rates remain high into the overnight \nperiod with a strengthening LLJ ahead of the front and bulk \nshear values increasing to 50-60kts along the front. The severe \nrisk looks marginal with a 15% chance of severe in our NW. Will \nneed to watch the parameter space Tuesday morning as the front \nmoves through northwest WI, but right now, do now expect a \nsevere threat in that area. \n\nA secondary front arrives Tuesday from the northwest, bringing \ndrier air with it, and a return to more mild temperatures. \n\nMid week:\n\nHigh pressure over the region for Wednesday with temperatures \nstaying in the 70s. Temperatures will slowly increase through \nthe end of the work week with intermittent rain chances possible\nas the upper level pattern turns more zonal.",
